  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/6/60/Tales_of_Suspense_Vol_1_52.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Crimson Dynamo is in Tony Stark's lab, still trying to perfect the laser light he was using before.&nbsp; He still seems to have become an ally of the US instead of the threat he originally was on behalf of the Reds, and for his part, his new employer has never had reason to regret his decision to take him on board and offer him a research position.\n\nAnd yet, Tony can see that he's had to don his old Crimson Dynamo armor to protect himself from the dangerous test he's now doing, and he rushes in to try and stop him from going forward with the experiment -- he mustn't risk his <i>life</i> for the sake of one experiment!&nbsp; He should <i>at least</i> add extra protection to his outfit.\n\nSatisfied that there was no nefarious motive, Tony Stark reflects on their one previous encounter, little knowing that the Reds in the East haven't forgotten Vanko, who they view as a traitor that must be eliminated!&nbsp; Their leader exclaims, \"This is a job for the <i>Black Widow</i>... and the man known as <i>Boris</i>!\"&nbsp; The agents so named are then brought in and given their assignment:&nbsp; eliminate the traitor Vanko and his American munitions-maker Stark, taking out Iron Man as well, if necessary.\n\nA few days later, the two show up at Stark's plant in Flushing, New York, posing as posing as \"foreign visitors.\"&nbsp; Tony Stark immediately takes interest in the beautiful \"Madame Natasha\" and invites her out to dinner, all while Boris is left to wander around the factory (secretly in search of the defector, Professor Vanko).\n\nBoris eludes a guard and eventually finds Vanko, who expresses his unwillingness to return to Russia, as the Americans have been \"good to me!\"&nbsp; But Boris subdues him and cues up a tape recording of a lecture Vanko gave in Moscow, all to make it sound as if Vanko is still in the lab, while in <i>reality</i>, Boris is actually hauling him away in a sack.\n\nA security guard Boris runs into hears Vanko's voice coming out of a loudspeaker, and is thus fooled by the deception.&nbsp; He allows Boris to pass, and it isn't long before the captured Vanko is delivered back into the hands of Russian agents in a Russian sub near the US shores.\n\nBoris then sneaks back into the factory and disguises himself as the Crimson Dynamo so that everyone will believe he is Vanko, who will appear to have gone rogue.&nbsp; He prepares to sabotage Stark's factory as well as take on Iron Man, as defeating <i>him</i> will be viewed back home as \"my greatest triumph!\"&nbsp; In moments, he begins destroying the lab equipment, which soon results in explosions that ultimately summon back the dining Stark.\n\nTony has to interrupt his date with Madame Natasha, but when they return to the sight of the damaged facility, he is startled at how cold her reaction is to seeing his plant in danger, but there's no time to ponder why because he soon races into action as Iron Man.\n\nInside, he encounters the Crimson Dynamo and mistakes him as Vanko, which allows Boris to attack him from behind.&nbsp; The stunned Iron Man soon becomes his prisoner, and Boris immediately rushes him to a waiting Russian submarine, all while believing that this unexpected prize will result in him getting a \"hero's welcome\" when they eventually return to the motherland.\n\nBut first... the <i>final</i> part of their mission remains:&nbsp; he and the Black Widow must find and \"finish off Anthony Stark...!\"\n\nWhen Iron Man awakens, he uses the current feeding an electric light bulb to recharge his drained armor, which allows him to smash through the sub's walls and eventually stumble upon the captured Professor Vanko.&nbsp; He now realizes the truth!&nbsp; Someone <i>else</i> is wearing the Crimson Dynamo armor!&nbsp; The two head back to the factory, and Iron Man engages in battle with Boris, who is still wearing the stolen armor.\n\nThe Black Widow attempts to run interference for Boris once it seems certain he will be defeated, which finally allows Iron Man to see that the two are working together.&nbsp; The momentary ruse works, and Boris moves in for the kill, but Professor Vanko provides Iron Man a needed distraction, ultimately sacrificing himself to destroy Boris using one of his own laser experiments.\n\nThe ensuing chaos allows the Black Widow to slip away unnoticed, all while Tony Stark swears that Vanko won't be forgotten, for \"he sacrificed his life to prove his loyalty to our nation!\"\n\nThough Madame Natasha got away, Stark knows that, in <i>reality</i>, she has no place to go.\n[BR] She may still be at large, but she must also live in constant fear... <b>for the Russians don't tolerate failure!</b>\n\n[CONTINUED: text=NEXT ISSUE:&nbsp; The Black Widow Returns!]",
  "grade_one_liner": "A routine spy plot is elevated by one final act of redemption.\nThe mysterious Black Widow provides little, yet leaves readers wanting more.",
  "subtitle": "More Questions Than Widow",
  "review_text": "As the first appearance of the Crimson Dynamo from last July's [LINK:1963:TOS_46_iron0|TOS #46|2026-12-10] had ended with the defection of Russian scientist Professor Vanko (and even showed him accepting a position in Stark's company), readers may well wonder why the cover shows the Crimson Dynamo attacking Iron Man.\n\n[HANG0] And indeed, the splash page wonders the same thing:\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=larger; text=Didn't he reform?&nbsp; Why then is he attacking the title hero?]\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=Good question!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nThe early pages establish that Vanko truly <i>does</i> seem to be working for the good of his employer, yet the narrative quickly reminds us that his defection is viewed with nothing short of contempt by the Russians.\n\nI have to pause a moment to laugh at the Russian leader's comment that, \"This is a job for the <i>Black Widow</i>... and the man known as <i>Boris</i>!\" [SPAN: severity=info; text=You'd think that there might be quite a <i>few</i> people in Russia with the name \"Boris,\"] and yet the leader seems to believe this identification alone is enough for his subordinates to know who he's talking about!\n[ASSET: center | BorisAndNatasha | width=large]\n\nWhoever these Russian agents are, their introduction establishes Boris as quite strong, while the Black Widow's abilities are still not revealed.&nbsp; They soon show up at Stark's factory posing as \"foreign visitors.\"\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[HANG0] The playboy Stark is immediately stricken with \"Madame Natasha's\" beauty, and he takes her out to dinner...\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=all while allowing Boris to roam about the factory unattended!]\n\nMakes you wonder about Stark sometimes... [SPAN: severity=warning; text=he must really have been <i>seriously</i> <i>taken</i> in by Natasha's beauty!]\n\n[ASSET: left | MoscowLectureTape | width=16%] [ASSET: right | DontDisturbVanko | width=16%] Page 6 shows Boris setting up a reel-to-reel tape recording of a lecture Professor Vanko gave in Moscow, all to fool the guard into believing that Vanko was still in the lab (when he was really in the sack Boris was holding).\n[BR]\n[BR] The guard overhears the speech... and lets Boris pass... [SPAN: severity=warning; text=but we see English words coming out of the loudspeaker!]&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold-italic; text=(Come on now!)]\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=larger; text=<i>Surely</i> Professor Vanko did not deliver his address in Moscow in <b><i>English</i></b>!]\n\nThis magazine seems particularly prone to this type of error, perhaps because Tony Stark tends to deal more in international affairs than most of the other Marvel characters, but this seems a [SPAN: severity=error; text=particularly heinous mistake!]\n\nFurthermore, any guard who lets a Russian visitor pass from a restricted area of the factory while holding a large sack... <i>well</i>... [SPAN: severity=info; text=let's just say Tony needs to hire better guards!]&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Wow!]\n\nBoris even sneaks back into the plant later and dons Vanko's Crimson Dynamo armor without anyone being the <i>wiser</i>!\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Tony, you <i>seriously </i>need to fire this security guard!)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | IronManIsFooled | width=11%] [ASSET: right | IronManCaptured | width=18%] What follows is a pretty routine surprise attack by Boris, the subsequent capture of Iron Man, who is then shown able to juice up his armor with nothing more than the normal current that powers a single light bulb [SPAN: severity=warning; text=(a remarkably efficient suit, considering what it usually does)], the discovery that Vanko is <i>also</i> a captive, meaning that the Crimson Dynamo must actually be the visiting Boris.\n[BR]\n[BR] In other words, [SPAN: severity=info; text=nothing extraordinary...]\n\n[ASSET: left | GullableFool | width=23%] The fact that Iron Man was momentarily fooled by Madame Natasha is sort of disappointing since, if you really think about it, if <b>Boris</b> was sent to sabotage Stark's factory, is it really outrageous to conclude that <b>Natasha</b> was <i>also</i> part of the plot?\n[BR]\n[BR] Yet Stark/Iron Man falls for the momentary deception and is taken out of action, ultimately making it necessary for Vanko to intervene and [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=save the day.]\n\nVanko gives his life to defeat Boris, who is wearing armor he knows is not strong enough to withstand the laser attack:\n[ASSET: center | VankoGivesHisLife | width=large]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nAs sad as this is, the ensuing chaos allows the Black Widow to escape -- yet this hardly feels like a victory.\n\nLater, Stark is quite right to point out <b>that she has no place to go</b>.&nbsp; The Russians don't tolerate failure, so she's left to live in constant fear.\n[ASSET: center | BlackWidowWillReturn | width=large]\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=But don't look now -- she will apparently be back next month, \"more deadly than ever!\"]\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThis was little more than a readable tale that leaves readers wondering about this <b>Black Widow</b> character, who the final page describes as being \"a real Mata Hari,\" yet the cover seems to advertise her as being much <i>more</i>, suggesting she is a force to be reckoned with.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=Pardon me while I scratch my head, but <b>what am I missing</b>?]\n\nFrom everything this story establishes, she is little more than a beautiful Russian spy.\n[HANG] * If she has any special abilities, the story never reveals them.\n[HANG] * Boris was admittedly strong, but why was <i>she</i> tagged for the mission?\n[HANG] * Is this something we might learn next month?\n\nThe promise of her return in the next issue certainly <i>implies</i> that there is more to her character that we're not yet privy to, so I suppose we'll soon see.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=I admit to being <b>intrigued</b>, but in the end, that's really the only reason the story elevates a hair above readable.]",
  "side_note": [
    "Page 4 establishes the Black Widow's name as \"Natasha,\" which is ironic, as the names \"Boris\" and \"Natasha\" may have been meant as a tribute to the [SPAN: fontsize=larger; fonttype=italic; text=Rocky and Bullwinkle Show], which, at the time of publication, was still being televised, having already been running for 3-4 years.\n\nIt's unlikely that we'll ever know this for sure, however....",
    "<b>Tales of the Watcher:</b>  \"The Failure!\"\n[BR] (by Stan Lee, Larry Lieber, and Paul Reinman)\n\nIn this issue's feature, we're told that in 300 years on Earth, there will be no wars, hunger, poverty or acts of violence, yet because humans must always strive for something, the accumulation of success and wealth moves into prominence.\n\nBut this is not true of Kenn Bentley and his girlfriend Valeria Reynolds, and this causes them to be outcasts of society.&nbsp; An alien ship shows up and demands that these two come with them!&nbsp; Nobody cares because Bentley is nothing but a big do-gooder -- nobody <i>important</i> was taken, they say.&nbsp; But the aliens were actually looking for someone unselfish enough to rule over them, so the pair become absolute monarchs of the \"greatest empire in the cosmos!\"\n\nThe Watcher then warns the readers with:&nbsp; \"Can any of us ever <i>really</i> know who is a failure, and who is not?\"\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Another moral warning to all readers everywhere!&nbsp; <i>Heh</i>)]"
  ],
  "ownership": "[ASSET: left | JeffsCopy | width=18%] I was fortunate enough to obtain a copy of this issue as part of the 2000 collection, which cost me under $20 bucks.\n[BR]\n[BR] About 19 years later, I pressed it and sent off to CGC for grading, coming back as 3.5.&nbsp; I sold it in early 2020 on eBay for $589, netting me over $400, which at the time was a nice return for my relatively low-grade copy.\n\nAt the time of this writing in 2023 (over three years later), similarly-graded copies were going for between $650-700, making my sale completely reasonable.&nbsp; (And while preparing this review for the website in Oct 2026, I checked eBay again, and it turns out that the prices for a 3.5 copy have now dropped <i>below</i> what I ultimately got, so in retrospect, I feel I got a pretty good price for it.)\n\nChronologically, it was the oldest CGC-graded comic book that I owned that was ultimately sold.&nbsp; (And yes, this was still the first time I actually <i>read</i> the issue!&nbsp; The pressing procedure did require me to handle each page, but I was burdened with massive inventory, and after a while, you largely tend to stop taking notice of the contents.)\n\n[ASSET: right | StoreStamp | width=12%] What's also interesting is that the copy had a store mark stamped on the top of the back cover that read \"JAN 8.\"&nbsp; Thus, it's <i>entirely conceivable</i> that the actual publication date is earlier than what the database says.",
  "highlight": "The Crimson Dynamo returns under false colors as \"Boris\" attacks Iron Man, an ultimate sacrifice is made, and the intriguing Black Widow leaves readers with more questions than answers."
}